---
// firma2-settings-notifications — which events email this account.
//
// LABELS CARRY THE WHOLE MEANING, so there is no description line under any of
// them. The first draft gave every switch a title plus a sentence explaining it,
// which needed a bespoke label/description/control row component — and every one
// of those sentences turned out to be the label again in more words. Writing the
// label as the event itself ("Stage changes on projects I sponsor") removes both
// the sentence and the component that existed to hold it. esa-switch-toggle's
// own `label` is then the switch's accessible name, which is the other half of
// why this shape is right: the lego renders its internal role="switch" button
// with no aria-label passthrough, so a switch whose name lives in a sibling
// element beside it has NO accessible name at all. The name has to be on the
// lego, and once it is, the sibling has nothing left to say.
//
// FOUR EVENTS, NOT A PAGE OF THEM. Each one is something a sponsor or program
// officer would plausibly want to hear about without opening the app — a record
// changed state, something is due, someone said something, and the weekly catch-up.
// Defaults are three on and one off: the digest is opt-in because it arrives
// whether or not anything happened, and a product that mails you to say nothing
// changed is how people learn to filter its mail.
//
// NOTHING HERE IS IRREVERSIBLE, which is why the whole section is a band of
// switches that can be read at a glance and flipped back — no confirmation, no
// save, no danger zone. The product brief reserves the room and the explanation
// for the irreversible, and there is none of it on this screen.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: esa-switch-toggle IS the control — form-associated, with the
// role="switch" semantics, the Space/Enter handling and the label all its own —
// and esa-card is the section. Neither is re-implemented; there is no bespoke
// track, thumb, or row. Walked the catalog for a settings-list or preference-row
// lego and there is none; esa-checkbox-group was the alternative considered and
// rejected — a checkbox group is one question with several answers submitted
// together, and these are four independent settings that each take effect on
// change. Beacon is not cloned on this machine. Layout is `.stack`, a hub
// primitive, so this component has no <style> block.

// The spoke's card-as-section convention: drops esa-card's internal rule under
// the title, because a bordered card stacked down a page already says where its
// section starts and the second line inside it draws a boundary the reader can
// see without it. Imported per component, the way all six project-detail
// sections import it, so the component carries the convention in isolation.
import '../shared/firma2-card-section.css';
import EsaCard from '@esa/ecology/esa-card.astro';

// The events, in the order a reader meets them: the record moved, something is
// owed, someone spoke, then the periodic summary.
const notifications = [
  { key: 'stage-change', label: 'Stage changes on projects I sponsor', on: true },
  { key: 'measure-due', label: 'Performance measure reports due soon', on: true },
  { key: 'comment', label: 'Comments on projects I sponsor', on: true },
  { key: 'digest', label: 'Weekly summary of my projects', on: false },
];
---
<EsaCard title="Email notifications">
  {/* `sm` rather than `md`: a switch row is a single line of text beside a 22px
      track, so it needs less air between siblings than the two-line term/value
      pairs in the Account card.

      LABEL AFTER THE TRACK — the lego's default, and kept rather than flipped.
      `label-position="before"` puts the switch at the end of each label, and the
      labels are four different lengths, so the tracks land in four different
      places down the card. Leading tracks give the section one straight edge and
      a column the eye can run down to read on/off, which is the only thing
      anyone comes to a notification list to do. */}
  <div class="stack" data-gap="sm">
    {notifications.map((n) => (
      <esa-switch-toggle
        name={n.key}
        label={n.label}
        checked={n.on ? true : undefined}
      ></esa-switch-toggle>
    ))}
  </div>
</EsaCard>

<script>
  // Registration only. These switches are a MOCK — nothing in this prototype
  // sends mail, so there is no change handler to write, and inventing a fake
  // persistence layer would make the section look more finished than it is. The
  // lego already handles its own checked state, keyboard and form value; the
  // import is the whole of the wiring it needs.
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-switch-toggle';
</script>
